== Availability

By design, a failed task, a broker outage or an identity outage loses no committed payment or event (AD-04, AD-06), but while the single identity task is replaced, purchases are refused. AWS tests demonstrated controlled task replacement, poison-message isolation and fail-closed payment initiation, within the configurations below. The database, the Valkey node and the NAT instance each run in one zone, so a zone failure still stops purchases, sign-ins or outbound calls (AISS-01). @tbl-availability-failures distinguishes observed recovery from designed behaviour.

*Application recovery.* On 16 October on AWS, two healthy commerce tasks ran in different zones. That deployment passed its smoke suite at 14:10 SGT only after an interrupted start and a manual repair of suspended scaling targets (#src("docs/swe5001/evidence/20261016-http-baseline-dashboard/README.md")[placement record], AISS-14). The ALB routes only to healthy targets, and ECS keeps the desired task count (AD-07, AD-08). The deployment circuit breaker rolls an unhealthy deployment back to the last completed one (#src("infra/swe5001/modules/ecs-service/main.tf")[ECS service module]). Configured, not tested: V6 (a bad release rolled back) was not run on AWS.

*V5 controlled task replacement.* On 13 November on AWS, V5 R3 passed. We offered 100 req/s of the browse mix (70% list, 30% detail) for 10 min to two commerce tasks, under the committed two-to-six policy and 12,000 target, and stopped one of the two tasks 181 s after it started, without changing the desired count. The load balancer first reported a healthy replacement 79.458 s later, within the 120 s limit; that is when we sampled it, not the exact moment it turned healthy. All 60,000 workload GETs and the setup GET succeeded, with no non-2xx response, browse error or dropped iteration. It used the released application `3a893036`, not the experimental V4 R5 image.

R2 showed what R3 needed. R2 ran commerce at 0.5 vCPU and 1 GiB with no slow start: the replacement was first seen healthy after 155 s, over the 120 s limit, and 120 requests failed. The errors came from the new task itself (cache timeouts and connection-pool waits while it warmed up), so for R3 we gave commerce 1 vCPU and 2 GiB and a 120 s warm-up; R3 tested the two changes together, not each alone. R3 also used a pool of five connections. Its task size and slow start were set for the session, and #pr(807) committed both to `dev` on 14 November (§3.2). R1’s workload was interrupted by a tooling error before the fault was armed (#published-evidence("20261114-v4-r5-live/v5-r3-pass.md")[V5 R3 record]).

