*Message recovery.* Broker-outage recovery remains designed, not tested on AWS. If SNS is unavailable when a team succeeds, the committed outbox row for its event stays in the database, and the relay retries publication to SNS up to ten times (`OutboxRelayService.MAX_ATTEMPTS`). SQS moves a message that fails five receives to its DLQ (AD-04, §3.2.3). Redelivery after a consumer commit is safe, because the consumer records each event in `sys_consumed_message`. Tested in CI, not on AWS: on 15 November the hosted run in @tbl-followup-15nov passed the selected ElasticMQ rollback, duplicate and poison, relay and expiry suites. Notifications can therefore arrive later, and exhausted relay retries or dead-letter messages need an operator (#src("backend/docs/adr/0007-cross-service-events-sns-sqs.md")[integration decision], #src("backend/blocbuy-infrastructure/src/main/java/com/blocbuy/notification/messaging/SqsNotificationEventConsumer.java")[queue consumer], #src("infra/swe5001/modules/messaging/main.tf")[queue and alarm configuration]).

*V7 poison-message drill.* On 14 November on AWS, V7 R4 passed. The production consumer received the poison message five times before SQS moved it to the DLQ; it left no consumed marker and sent no notification. A valid control message sent at the same time produced one consumed marker and one notification to its intended recipient. The DLQ alarm fired and reset, and we deleted only the poison message. Injection to alarm took 233.130 s, and the sampled DLQ arrival to alarm 158.232 s, both within the 300 s limits; the second is not exact DLQ enqueue latency. The alarm email reached one of us at 10:12 SGT; the inbox shows minutes only, so it gives no exact delivery latency (#published-evidence("20261114-v7-r4-live/README.md")[V7 R4 record], #published-evidence("20261114-v7-r4-live/acceptance.json")[acceptance]).

For this run we shortened the consumer's visibility timeout from 60 s to 10 s. The image, resources, workers and queue and scaling policies were unchanged, and the source queue stayed at 60 s. We restored the 60 s setting by 10:20:39 SGT and confirmed it in a fresh readback at 10:31:54 SGT. We have not shown that the production 60 s setting meets the 300 s target: R1, at 60 s, took 515.129 s, well over the 300 s limit, and failed, and R2 and R3 failed during setup, before any message was sent.

*Identity outage.* Our first AWS attempt, on 13 November (identity attempt R1), failed. START returned HTTP 200 instead of the expected 500, and recovery took 217.828 s, over the 180 s gate. The service count reached zero before the old application had shut down, so R1 never made identity unavailable (#published-evidence("20261113-aws-validation/identity-r1.md")[R1 record]). After R1 we narrowed the acceptance for this follow-up to payment initiation: it must fail while identity is down and succeed once identity is back. For R11 on 15 November (@tbl-followup-15nov) we waited for the application to shut down. START then failed while identity was down and succeeded once it was back. We did not apply R1’s 180 s recovery gate to R11; identity health returned after 167.578 s, which is not the payment endpoint's recovery time. R11 covers payment initiation only; card confirmation, signed-webhook failure and redelivery, and replay remain untested on AWS. (R11 is the eleventh revision of the 15 November procedure; R1–R10 of that procedure were setup revisions and refused launches, not executed faults, and are unrelated to the 13 November attempt.)

